// AuthenticationRequired returns the missing-session refusal. It is separate
// from PermissionDenied because the two ask the caller for different things:
// one says sign in, the other says you have signed in and still may not.
func AuthenticationRequired() Definition { return authenticationRequired }

// InvalidCredentials returns the sign-in refusal. It is deliberately one code
// for an address with no account, a wrong password, and a disabled account:
// each of those is a fact an unauthenticated caller would like to learn, and a
// sign-in form is the cheapest place to ask. Which one happened is in the
// audit record.
func InvalidCredentials() Definition { return invalidCredentials }

// SecondFactorRequired returns the unfinished sign-in. It is not a failure the
// caller should retry with different credentials: the password was right, and
// the response carries the challenge to answer.
func SecondFactorRequired() Definition { return secondFactorRequired }

// TooManyAttempts returns the throttle refusal.
func TooManyAttempts() Definition { return tooManyAttempts }

// InvitationInvalid returns the enrollment refusal. Like InvalidCredentials it
// is one code for every reason — unknown, already accepted, expired, revoked —
// because an invitation token is a credential and telling a holder which of
// their guesses was structurally valid is how the guessing gets cheaper.
func InvitationInvalid() Definition { return invitationInvalid }

// PasswordRejected returns the password-policy refusal. It is a 422 rather
// than a 400 because the request is syntactically fine and semantically
// refused, and the caller can fix it by choosing another password.
func PasswordRejected() Definition { return passwordRejected }

